#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130023} :activity/id #uuid "c092012f-6280-5531-b39f-09c65d20ea5d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130371 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the region that lies between the curves ${\\color{red}y=f(x)}$ and ${\\color{blue}y=g(x)}$ over a closed interval $[a,b]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a45abf7904382a16daaa38ff617a9487b5c274cf65ef44f89e7feb0c32c496e0.png)\n\nThis region is an example of a **type I region**. Generally, a type I region is any region bounded by two vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ and two curves $y=g(x)$ and $y=f(x),$ where $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions. \n\nUsing set notation, a type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq x  \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn a type I region, any vertical line drawn through the region should intersect the region in a *single* interval, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7282efbe2c9244c77ce6bbbeca047ee3ab29dd2fc90e0d2440c519328cb5973.png)\n\nBy contrast, the following region is *not* a type I region since there is a vertical line whose intersection with the region results in two *separate* line segments. In other words, the vertical line enters and leaves the region more than once.\n\n![](images/a2/a26c7588e297a8af35f1e709210596a94f3fb4bd84e8237dc1462f48b961630e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "2658cc5e-d169-5ff8-871c-4ccaab428eb7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3854 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0b518252-fe1c-57d7-bc6f-6a00d3addac9" :step/math-academy-id 22589 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128986 :question/id #uuid "9cbb7321-753d-5253-8b64-072e3cc227bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6101"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050641}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435896}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"} :step/id #uuid "1b6899de-a336-56a9-809a-31cc01aeebb8" :step/math-academy-id 22590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130372 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the region that lies between the curves ${\\color{red}x=f(y)}$ and ${\\color{blue}x=g(y)}$ over a closed interval $[a,b]$ on the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf2a2a41d63f21b279215fd1aa8d7361eb638e831b970bb3de861673f67612e6.png)\n\nThis region is an example of a **type II region**. In general, a type II region is any region that is bounded by two horizontal lines $y=a$ and $y=b,$ and two curves $x=g(y)$ and $x=f(y),$ where $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nUsing set notation, a type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq y  \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn a type II region, any horizontal line drawn through the region should intersect the region in a *single* interval, as shown below.\n\n![](images/56/56019e8cc2b1146b99fa6f6532356281fdb847c342c25c63f843ddee4f8cb197.png)\n\nBy contrast, the following region is *not* a type II region since there is a horizontal line whose intersection with the region results in two *separate* line segments. In other words, the horizontal line enters and leaves the region more than once.\n\n![](images/a7/a7c1ead1274250f7184b132eff58a3028ce648715dcbfa7c7046077fd4e680ef.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d089f833-c09a-5229-9be0-8cffa2510d60" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3855 :tutorial/title "Type II Regions"} :step/id #uuid "4858561d-380d-50d9-901d-e33ce219f2d6" :step/math-academy-id 22591 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130026}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128987 :question/id #uuid "cce0a7b2-7b25-5da6-8aa2-cbe8a40cd522" :question/math-academy-id "e-6102"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85335de6-e1dc-5a2f-b30a-806627759030" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435897}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type II Regions"} :step/id #uuid "952b663b-02bc-55c3-b391-c854df6de6fb" :step/math-academy-id 22592 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130027}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130373 :tutorial/content "In general, a given region can be type I, type II, both, or neither.\n\nFor example, let's consider the region in the coordinate plane bounded between the circle ${\\color{blue}x^2+y^2=2}$ and the lines ${\\color{red}x=-1},$ ${\\color{red}x=1},$ ${\\color{red}y=-1}$, and ${\\color{red}y=1},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db43502c2b096c912ac38d297c3a06e7dcbf4945af6fbc7f5e365a40d11fcde8.png)\n\nThere is a vertical line (for instance, the $y$-axis) whose intersection with the region results in two separate line segments. In other words, the vertical line enters and leaves the region more than once. So, this region *cannot* be a type I region.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1dca3b3015da15f554d95dae7add65aeee0b83a0075ec5295716a3e86d71f5.png)\n\nSimilarly, there is a horizontal line (for instance, the $x$-axis) whose intersection with the region results in two separate line segments. In other words, the horizontal line enters and leaves the region more than once. So, this region *cannot* be a type II region.\n\n![](images/82/82720f4b69fc3e63bbf54cb22d60299035faf91a6021f09d41e9f62dad571581.png)\n\nIn conclusion, this region is *neither* type I nor type II.\n\nLet's now look at an example that is *both* type I and type II." :tutorial/id #uuid "09369aae-8943-5475-9739-20e6619b16d7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3856 :tutorial/title "Regions that are Neither Type I Nor Type II"} :step/id #uuid "eafe2c8b-e7b1-59e6-9468-2c731ee86e98" :step/math-academy-id 22593 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130028}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128988 :question/id #uuid "cd9ce5bf-1663-5110-8aaa-8f0f47d7d30a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6103"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050294}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446088}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"} :step/id #uuid "3b78b691-c694-59d1-b1ef-5ea8a77ad679" :step/math-academy-id 22594}] :activity/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129327}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406984} :activity/id #uuid "3da669c9-a862-59b7-84e5-f7ce7ac62942" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5bce626-d9bb-5dbb-905a-7daed41e086c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a2/a216b288b70301fb14b8f1219b909edb69ab77b08c29460b5522abc2025ab494.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393196} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d47ae6-371d-5f7f-a924-edcd6ec62dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393197} {:answer/id #uuid "693ea9c7-f1ff-5a1c-b288-3ea683ff953d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/20/205fdc7a29c94d36b5bd8847a2600c41d7e3fd2ac0fd92947dddcb241bdae012.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393198} {:answer/id #uuid "349c2643-543b-501a-b8d3-7af986d4a0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/254691f56d0586b2cbfd75a3fce34ecc1062b0f0fde538d3885860ed1b71555c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393199} {:answer/id #uuid "d7836927-19a4-54a2-aabf-fc9fd48ada96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ed/edbcc3bcef921ef736f9bd31bec56f69736c632bf835cf4c9c1c46026fda8083.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393200}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393196} :answer-field/id #uuid "7683d677-08f6-57b3-b924-04b4d1ba5f5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "13be6924-14b7-5b8e-8469-18a97723bd20" :question/math-academy-id "q-72120" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/e4/e472070f17c47e78024217ed750690b88de87f3849818368dda1dde5d91d90d4.png)"} :step/id #uuid "0868f274-6d6e-5256-97a9-564c4f1a8169" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099efdac-6222-5744-b3c1-1dad27d41b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336117} {:answer/id #uuid "2f51b9b3-9edb-5ad5-a10c-52cfdfd9e751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6c/6ccd325f24c9550f82a7063623db1cae0212a07f1c938b882c6707d35a6eee29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336484} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4f7dba-2306-55b0-ad53-f7348ae38b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5a/5a14be084ba22e33b636465156276fc489904a650077612c15fdec3634f8d902.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336855} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f500b5-47d1-5c54-82d7-03d7175ff4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5e/5e41509dcb689a5f16822eea9b8700171a74124789e12990ee4fd292d52aa2ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337919} {:answer/id #uuid "f39c32b9-aa95-5261-a245-c6851bf03b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/81/81b206d34bfa12b8623329616368ea3e6a978cf7963dbe5f09b23cafffb76dae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336484} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0492a08-2d29-5eca-af88-ac3ff00ea955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "efbcfea6-0f26-55f3-9790-4f12e94364d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-72049" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/7c/7c159c025f0b7ae4bfa8984f637a492cda21ec2dd39e87be724324e1334c06a3.png)"} :step/id #uuid "11bc950f-28ab-5ada-8524-78d17fbdb0fc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0747cf82-408b-58c2-b5d7-dcefd0ed68ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435869} {:answer/id #uuid "17567c73-9403-53e0-b46b-a5ceadd85060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435871} {:answer/id #uuid "429ef4b8-fbc0-567d-af67-084931824074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le y\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435875} {:answer/id #uuid "4b704cda-a7e0-5c8b-9cf6-66429dfd1ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435876} {:answer/id #uuid "a709f16c-6935-5b16-8b38-88908d6454cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435884} :answer-field/id #uuid "257fac2f-8a02-59c7-aca1-ef19cbb1795a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef441fce-64e9-5f90-b9fb-0d44af6b333e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72143" :question/problem "![](images/76/768046952484d181a404486547db84882424d98da4f677254484e1083580a39b.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nThe function $x=y$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we have $0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/d5/d55cb7ea67d9865368c744577bb4a4b22a3635e8e62869ee65e945612d6c0e98.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=y$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\sqrt{y}.$ So, we have $y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1c227960-e1d3-5f07-b75a-31b153d00a8a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406987}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cb3d1e7-cf6a-54b2-a095-b9fdc68a8d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467926} {:answer/id #uuid "cab149b0-2702-528d-b56e-23d4232647f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467927} :answer-field/id #uuid "368ddd66-9d80-586f-9e2a-9ab70f075343" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468684} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ea3c229-904d-5a2f-8d07-a00dada077bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467928} {:answer/id #uuid "7d445f31-2d12-5062-a2a4-99e2b64c0491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467928} :answer-field/id #uuid "4edea166-3bfd-535f-b98e-b624be2d49a5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468685} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a94e95bb-c951-576f-aa60-7b79a87fb894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467930} {:answer/id #uuid "1541c926-bd73-5e47-854d-d4e15738f59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467930} :answer-field/id #uuid "88bc4663-71c0-5111-9c92-a5bd0ddc37dc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f581ab20-43e5-59a8-ac58-914378a7361e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72186" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ and the line $y=2-|x|?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is a type I region\n- **II.** It is a type II region\n- **III.** It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/6f/6f35161e7efbd53c9ef558ffe88d8e046771128965a9fdd02143ae37a9b130ea.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,2-|x|\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- The horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/76/769a521cd6b05ecf05052da382e40295b26be66fe56e4eff7c6940c9032f2f0b.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} :step/id #uuid "52cd1fa6-d29c-562d-9d88-cade3cec5a66"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b532929-031f-53ba-845b-1b69f35ae5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473278} {:answer/id #uuid "da5ae196-6ddc-5f90-8faf-ce02251fa644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473279}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473279} :answer-field/id #uuid "8261d6df-4d7c-5438-ab24-46a9a778e5ea" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474034} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff2626a4-af3b-558c-898b-e325dcd7c8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473280} {:answer/id #uuid "74014f19-619f-54ff-9fc7-8f16f269680a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473281} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f34c2c1-88e7-5e59-bf72-b638136d3b12" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474035} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d131820-9e7d-5679-bbf5-8ff122bec3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473282} {:answer/id #uuid "38a132f3-eea1-5c88-b25c-151a9517cd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473282} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f148b1b-a08c-5f92-88d7-fab284af6a47" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d779a1b-dcae-59f0-822c-7860f9483ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-72160" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt[3]{x},$ in the first quadrant?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is a type I region\n- **II.** It is a type II region\n- **III.** It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{3}\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\, & ⟹\\,x={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/89/899357795c6d5b81d3cb619d71493ad787645697fe1f2fcecda78c1b875f388e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{3}\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."} :step/id #uuid "fd242e61-69b5-5bfd-bc83-d7379d3f45c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406986}}] :activity/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406975}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429979} :activity/id #uuid "d57582b7-233f-54ba-bba8-bc7760d18c20" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177be529-dbfe-5550-a65b-a03b4c722a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le {e}^{x-1},\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429962} {:answer/id #uuid "69b6c086-4549-55a7-9eba-d0041a274109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le e,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429966} {:answer/id #uuid "ad13df4f-7bd9-5a03-86d7-02175fdb12da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le e,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429968} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dacd0b-ebeb-5ba4-b6db-4ab0dd2f71e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le {e}^{x-1},\\,1\\le x\\le 2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429970} {:answer/id #uuid "dfdb17ab-530e-5006-a6b1-b68bfc5121a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le {e}^{x-1},\\,0\\le x\\le 2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429966} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1839b45-678e-5716-ac62-7bfdda74ad68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5cace0f5-d8d3-5cc8-93c4-64d577bf2d3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72344" :question/problem "![](images/40/4066b07ace047e9a7ce813ed70dd6648649a192d648ed810fa96b5146d2c5581.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x-1}\\,⟹\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\n$$\n\nThe function $x=0$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=e.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le e.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/68/680842905f6e308d3c438063769bde9854401a300371452d1c0735bb7d272a02.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=0$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le e,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3390ee50-86eb-5461-9633-3fe230269903" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28e2c148-6ee6-5f8c-9b2b-442fe96cf085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467920} {:answer/id #uuid "90143807-a74a-57ee-aa16-a1ee057a16c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467921} :answer-field/id #uuid "2de9075c-99a1-540e-b78d-991060565cb7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468681} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8488a4b-f9b2-5a2f-aeff-132fefe2716f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467922} {:answer/id #uuid "1a62f2dd-5c52-5ce6-8102-f840944d8ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467923} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5389031-03cb-5727-b801-66a146d9f193" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468682} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25b7c356-4e59-589f-9d6a-565ed0873aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467924} {:answer/id #uuid "58cf1eb8-5878-59da-b070-f3f00fcdcb15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467924} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb366d99-7744-5bfb-8897-cc8aae3154da" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0bcd8034-22f3-5298-b8d3-b69cdcbc84fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72185" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region inside the circumference ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is a type I region\n- **II.** It is a type II region\n- **III.** It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/09/0960063edcce8ccd55dc2f77147c4a4982645c180e8b998d7e520c65131ea045.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."} :step/id #uuid "4eb88ece-6f0a-54bd-b308-acf69dcf4cf6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429980}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e353de59-45ed-5d3e-a3c7-c62ccb8bfee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/32/32e6ed07f3229c8e6016d12dd35c42597888662fbc909f53bc07e495a73958e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389596} {:answer/id #uuid "189f5878-608c-523c-8af3-61b336e7fea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389597} {:answer/id #uuid "cae29720-9bdf-5675-b7dc-87fb52dc9fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/36/36275eef6e634c83c614626bc2af6b97cd5bafddc8c02b64ba2a1aa7ea983317.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389598} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e89d64-16cb-5f6e-924f-288db18c0bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/de/de643820e7690a10eae2e7de2e2cb974700996b36130af4f45c446640b49dcc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389599} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e82089-b886-5a96-8c4e-a2c7d256121e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5a/5ac45c6757e187796674ca4beda4ff73c60835b401df302bfd8a91fa5c98b68c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389600}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389599} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e761f31-f26b-54c2-90ef-d2fbb7506ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2da77bc4-04e1-56ef-a7c8-2c62ea7852fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-168140" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/1b/1bee7870760a7c267ca83806f04b3fa8b933891fa5e5524ce76ee09f2c7c98e0.png)"} :step/id #uuid "89758ef5-24c5-59a5-a86a-6a96520e9970"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad9fabe-6ac2-5a64-ad33-5fdd7127093f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429963} {:answer/id #uuid "1d70f350-c29f-598f-92ec-fbca126bbc5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/78/781f679784282a55e4ff6b7a5537047ef2f45c8346cc9d1ed341407ecb8f8a38.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429964} {:answer/id #uuid "52b32bb1-058f-52fc-8bbd-90d501685805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ba/ba9994e94193fb25ac13c50e22e17c571ef563420325a02b593a37c4634e3998.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429965} {:answer/id #uuid "795e2b60-0fa8-5665-b329-1cbd1b3e42fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/69/69de9afdd158f32add63cc93e37b12ed0e3a80